# Temporary files always get cleaned up

Scratch this project writes runs to gigabytes - client archives, MPQ extracts, DBC exports, armory
image staging. Nothing may be left behind for the operating system to deal with.

## Production code

Create scratch with `TempWorkspace` (`backend/AzerothPlatform.Infrastructure/Services/Shared/`) and
bind it with `using`, so the compiler owns the cleanup:

```csharp
using var scratch = TempWorkspace.CreateDirectory("patch-import");
using var upload = TempWorkspace.CreateFile("addon-upload", ".zip");
```

Do not call `Path.GetTempPath()` or `Path.GetTempFileName()` directly. Scratch created outside
`TempWorkspace.Root` is invisible to `TempWorkspaceSweeper`, so a process killed mid-run leaks it
permanently rather than for at most six hours.

Scratch that must live next to the data it works on (a stack root, a Docker mount) stays where it is,
but its removal still belongs in a `finally`, never on the happy path alone.

## Tests

Use `TempDir` (`backend/AzerothPlatform.Tests/TestSupport/`) with `using`, or hold one in a field on an
`IDisposable` test class. A `Directory.Delete` at the end of a test body does not run when an assertion
fails, and the suite creates hundreds of these per run.

## Agent scratch

Files written to explore, reproduce, or measure something - probe scripts, sample projects, dumps -
are deleted before the turn that created them ends, whether or not the experiment worked.
